// FAIL CANARY. Exact i32 multiplication must reject when its result cannot be
// proved representable, including when it is nested inside another operand.
//
// `(self.a * self.b) % 7` with a = b = 100000, so a*b = 10_000_000_000, which
// overflows i32 (max ~2.1e9). The multiply is a NESTED value operand feeding
// the `%`, never stored to an i32 slot in between, so the two backends treat
// the intermediate width differently:
//   - NATIVE x64: wraps the nested i32 multiply to 32 bits (1410065408), then
//     1410065408 % 7 = 3  -> exits 73.
//   - INTERPRETER: computes the multiply at full width (10e9), then
//     10e9 % 7 = 4        -> exits 74.
//
// This is NOT auto-caught by the differential oracle because the canary is not
// registered as a RUN canary (and must not be until the semantics is decided).
// A STORED intermediate (`let p: i32 = a*b; let r = p % 7;`) does NOT diverge:
// the interpreter wraps on write to `p`, so both give 3. Only the unstored
// nested intermediate differs.
//
// MAINTAINER RULING (2026-06-14): integer overflow/wrap on a primitive is a
// COMPILE ERROR by default -- arithmetic is "exact/proven" (chapter 5
// "The likely default is exact/proven arithmetic"; wrapping/saturating are
// explicit opt-ins). So THIS PROGRAM IS ILL-FORMED: `100000 * 100000` cannot be
// proven in-range for i32 and must be REJECTED at compile time. Neither runtime
// result (3 or 4) is "correct"; the divergence is a SYMPTOM of accepting an
// unprovable operation. The real gap is the missing overflow proof-check (the
// exact-arithmetic obligation is not yet enforced).
//
// When that checker lands this becomes a FAIL canary (expected compile error).
// To get a DEFINED runtime result you opt the operands into wrapping/saturating
// arithmetic explicitly; both backends must then agree on that defined behavior
// (today they would still diverge -- see the integer-arm width note in the
// smell doc). NOTE (not yet settled): chapter 5 spells the opt-in as arithmetic
// *policies* (exact / wrapping / saturating / trap / checked); the maintainer
// described it as *domains* (`u32 in Wrapping`). The spelling is a frontier.
//
// Until the overflow checker exists, tracked as a CurrentlyAccepts pending
// canary (it still compiles). Exit codes are diagnostic only (73 = native's
// wrapped 3, 74 = interpreter's full-width 4, 75 = neither).

use omega::language::std::console;
data Main { console: Console; a: i32; b: i32; }
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.a = 100000;
    self.b = 100000;
    let r: i32 = (self.a * self.b) % 7;
    transition r == 3 {
        true -> three()
        _ -> not_three()
    }
    state three(&mut self) { self.console.exit_process(73); }
    state not_three(&mut self) {
        transition r == 4 {
            true -> four()
            _ -> other()
        }
    }
    state four(&mut self) { self.console.exit_process(74); }
    state other(&mut self) { self.console.exit_process(75); }
}
